Q1  the identity  sum_f C_f (x) S_f = V I  on 20000 random tetrahedra
    (if true, Green-Gauss with the TRUE face value is linear-exact
     on any mesh, so any error is the interpolation's)
    worst relative deviation: 2.900e-16   (eps = 2.220e-16)
    VERDICT: IDENTITY HOLDS to rounding

Q2/Q3/Q4  a cell and four neighbours, an EXACT linear field, three methods

  built so skewness varies: each neighbour centroid is placed off the
  line through the face centroid by a controlled amount.

   skewness    Gauss(true phi_f)   Gauss(interpolated)   least-squares   LSQ cond
   --------    -----------------   -------------------   -------------   --------
       0.00             1.37e-12              2.52e-12        5.81e-11   282180.4
       0.25             7.09e-13              1.89e+01        1.08e-11   131563.1
       0.50             7.95e-13              4.77e+01        5.57e-11   170024.2
       1.00             1.02e-12              1.12e+02        7.94e-11   671145.3
       2.00             1.65e-12              1.27e+02        1.40e-10   1467940.7
       4.00             9.56e-13              4.34e+02        3.93e-11   277292.5
       6.50             8.32e-13              4.53e+02        1.04e-10   673707.9

READ THE COLUMNS, not the headline:
  Gauss(true phi_f)     stays at rounding at EVERY skewness -> Gauss itself
                        is linear-exact; Q1's identity is why
  Gauss(interpolated)   grows with skewness -> the INTERPOLATION is what
                        breaks linear exactness, and a skewness
                        correction needs the gradient it is computing
  least-squares         stays at rounding at every skewness, with no
                        interpolation at all
